  "what_to_watch": [],
  "linkedin_post": "Hyperliquid just passed Bybit, Kraken, and OKX in open interest. Not in volume. In open interest. That is a different metric entirely, and the one that matters for who actually lives on a platform versus who routes through it.\n\nThe 80% Q3 jump to $191 million revenue came with stock-linked trading volumes, which means the product is bleeding into non-crypto-native users. That is the migration story everyone has been underwriting for three years, and it is happening in perpetuals first because the CEX risk premium—counterparty, jurisdictional, custody—compounds fastest in leveraged positions.\n\nSame day, the Bank of England, MAS, and Thailand's central bank close a FX settlement trial with a 2028 live interface target. Tokenized settlement infrastructure gets a hard date. These are not the same market. They are not even the same investor base. But they share one thing: both repriced today, and neither was priced in yesterday.\n\nThe test for the Hyperliquid thesis is whether open interest growth converts to sticky maker volume when funding rates compress. The test for the central bank thesis is whether 2028 arrives with live rails or another pilot. We are underwriting both, but separately."
}